# 3. Containerfile vs Image vs Container

These three are often confused.

## Step 1 — Write a Containerfile

```dockerfile
FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi:latest

CMD ["sleep","infinity"]
```

This file contains only **instructions**.

---

## Step 2 — Build an Image

```bash
podman build -t myimage .
```

Result:

```text
Containerfile
      │
      ▼
 Image
```

---

## Step 3 — Run a Container

```bash
podman run myimage
```

Result:

```text
Image
   │
   ▼
Container
```

Complete workflow:

```text
Containerfile
      │
      ▼
Build
      │
      ▼
Image
      │
      ▼
Run
      │
      ▼
Container
```

---

# 4. Understanding Build Context (`.`)

Many beginners think:

```bash
podman build -t myimage .
```

means

> Use this file.

It actually means

> **Use this directory as the build context.**

Podman automatically searches inside that directory for:

```
Containerfile
```

or

```
Dockerfile
```

If your file has another name:

```
mycontainerfile
```

Specify it explicitly:

```bash
podman build -t myimage -f mycontainerfile .
```

---

# 5. Build vs Run

These are two completely different operations.

## Build

```bash
podman build
```

Produces:

```text
Image
```

---

## Run

```bash
podman run
```

Produces:

```text
Container
```

Easy way to remember:

| Command | Creates |
|----------|----------|
| `podman build` | Image |
| `podman run` | Container |

