# 8. Does Podman Create Container Directories?

Suppose the image contains only:

```dockerfile
FROM registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi

CMD ["sleep","infinity"]
```

No directories exist:

```
/opt/incoming

/opt/outgoing
```

Now run:

```bash
podman run \
-v /opt/files:/opt/incoming \
-v /opt/processed:/opt/outgoing
```

Podman automatically prepares those mount points inside the running container.

```text
Host
│
├── /opt/files
└── /opt/processed

        │
        ▼

Container
│
├── /opt/incoming
└── /opt/outgoing
```

The **image itself is never modified.**

---

# 9. Images are Immutable

Images are read-only templates.

```text
           Image
             │
      ┌──────┼──────┐
      ▼      ▼      ▼
   Container A
   Container B
   Container C
```

Suppose you delete a file inside Container A.

Only Container A changes.

```text
Container A ✔ Modified

Container B ✔ Unchanged

Container C ✔ Unchanged

Image ✔ Unchanged
```

Every container gets its own writable layer.

---

# 10. Why `sleep infinity`?

Without a long-running process:

```dockerfile
CMD ["echo","Hello"]
```

Container lifecycle:

```text
Starts

↓

Prints Hello

↓

Stops
```

With:

```dockerfile
CMD ["sleep","infinity"]
```

Container lifecycle:

```text
Starts

↓

Keeps Running

↓

Can be Managed

↓

Can Generate systemd

↓

Can Auto-start
```

This is why **sleep infinity** is commonly used for EX200 practice.

